hadiths[22]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17788","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Nasr, from a man, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade plucking the tails of horses and their manes and their forelocks, and said: Their tails are their fly-whisks, and their manes are their warmth, and in their forelocks good is tied until the Day of Resurrection."
"17789","Ismail ibn Umar and Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, both saying: Hariz narrated to us, from Sharahbil Ibn Shufa al-Rahabi who said: I heard Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, the companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and Hasan said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No Muslim man whose three children die before puberty but they meet him at the eight gates of Paradise, whichever he wills he enters."
"17790","Abdullah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Nasr, from a man of Banu Sulaym, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade trimming the manes of horses and plucking their tails and trimming their forelocks, and said: As for their tails, they are their fly-whisks, and as for their manes, they are their warmth, and as for their forelocks, good is tied in them."
"17791","Isam ibn Khalid narrated to us, Abu Abdullah al-Hasan ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nasij al-Hadrami narrated to me who said: Utba ibn Abd narrated to me who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered fighting, and a man among his companions was shot with an arrow. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This has become binding. And they said when he ordered them to fight: Then, O Messenger of Allah, we shall not say as the Children of Israel said: Go, you and your Lord, and fight, indeed we are sitting here. But go, you and your Lord, and fight, indeed we are with you fighting."
"17792","Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Amir ibn Zayd al-Bukali, that he heard Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami say: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about the Basin, and he mentioned Paradise. Then the Bedouin said: In it is fruit. He said: Yes, and in it is a tree called Tuba. He mentioned something I do not know what it is. He said: Which trees of our land does it resemble. He said: It resembles nothing of the trees of your land. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you been to Sham. He said: No. He said: It resembles a tree in Sham called the walnut, growing upon a single trunk with its top spreading. He said: How great is its root. He said: If a young she-camel among the camels of your family set out, it would not encompass its root until its collarbone breaks from old age. He said: In it are grapes. He said: Yes. He said: How great is the bunch. He said: A month journey for the pied crow without stumbling. He said: How great is the berry. He said: Did your father ever slaughter a great he-goat among his sheep. He said: Yes. He said: He skinned its hide and gave it to your mother. He said: Make for us of it a bucket. He said: Yes. The Bedouin said: That berry will satiate me and the people of my house. He said: Yes, and most of your clan."
"17793","Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Nasr ibn Alqama narrated to me who said: A man of Banu Sulaym narrated to me, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not cut the forelocks of horses, for in them is blessing. And do not trim their manes, for it is their warmth. And do not cut their tails, for they are their fly-whisks."
"17794","Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us who said: Hariz narrated to us, from Sharahbil Ibn Shufa who said: I heard Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No servant whose three children die before puberty but they meet him at the eight gates of Paradise, whichever he wills he enters."
"17795","Hisham ibn Said narrated to us, Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nasij al-Hadrami narrated to me, and he had met Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and those below them, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions: Stand and fight. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, and we shall not say as the Children of Israel said to Musa, peace be upon him: Go, you and your Lord, and fight, indeed we are sitting here. But go, you and your Lord, O Muhammad, and fight, and we are with you fighting."
"17796","Hisham ibn Said narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Hadrami narrated to us who said: Abdullah ibn Nasij al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions: Stand and fight. He said: A man was shot with an arrow. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This has become binding."
"17797","Haywa ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Baqiyya narrated to me, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Utba ibn Abd, that he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, curse the people of Yemen, for strong is their might, many their number, fortified their forts. He said: No. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the non-Arabs. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When they pass by you driving their women, carrying their sons upon their shoulders, they are from me and I am from them."
"17798","Haywa and Yazid ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, both saying: Baqiyya narrated to us, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Ibn Amr al-Sulami, from Utba ibn Abd al-Sulami, that he told them that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: How was the beginning of your affair, O Messenger of Allah. He said: My nursing mother was of Banu Sad ibn Bakr. I went out, I and a son of hers, with lambs of ours, and we took no provisions with us. I said: O my brother, go and bring us provisions from our mother. My brother went and I stayed with the lambs. Two white birds came, as if they were vultures. One of them said to his companion: Is it he. He said: Yes. They rushed to me and took me and laid me on my back, and split my belly, then took out my heart and split it, and took out of it two black clots. One of them said to his companion. Yazid said in his hadith: Bring me snow water. So they washed my belly with it. Then he said: Bring me hail water. So they washed my heart with it. Then he said: Bring me tranquility. So he scattered it in my heart. Then one of them said to his companion: Stitch it. So he stitched it and sealed upon it with the seal of prophethood. And Haywa said in his hadith: Stitch it. So he stitched it, and seal upon it with the seal of prophethood. One of them said to his companion: Put him in a scale, and put a thousand of his nation in a scale. I looked at the thousand above me, fearing that some of them would fall upon me. He said: If his nation were weighed with him, he would outweigh them. Then they went and left me, and I feared with severe fear. Then I went to my mother and told her what I met, and she feared for me that I was possessed. She said: I seek Allah for you. She saddled a camel of hers and put me. And Yazid said: She carried me upon the saddle and rode behind me, until we reached my mother. She said: Have I fulfilled my trust and my covenant. And she told her of what I met, but that did not frighten her. She said: I saw light come out of me by which the palaces of Sham were lit."
"17799","Haywa ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Baqiyya narrated to us, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Utba ibn Abd who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man were dragged upon his face from the day he was born till the day he dies old, in seeking the pleasure of Allah, Almighty and Majestic, he would deem it little on the Day of Resurrection."
"17800","Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Muhammad ibn Abi Umayra, and he was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: If a servant fell upon his face from the day he was born till he dies old, in obedience to Allah, he would deem that day little, and would wish to be returned to this world so as to increase of reward and recompense."
"17801","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd, from 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He said: The martyrs and those who died of plague will come, and the people of the plague will say: We are martyrs. Then it will be said: Look; if their wounds are like the wounds of the martyrs, flowing blood with the scent of musk, then they are martyrs. And they will find them to be so."
"17802","Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us: Abu Humayd al-Ru'ayni narrated to me, he said: Yazid Dhu Misr informed me, he said: I came to 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami and said: O Abu al-Walid, I went out seeking sacrificial animals and found nothing pleasing to me except a tharma' [a sheep missing its front teeth], so what do you say? He said: Why did you not bring it to me? I said: Glory be to Allah, is it permissible for you and not permissible for me? He said: Yes, you doubt and I do not doubt. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade al-musfarrah, and al-musta'salah whose horn is uprooted from its root, and al-bakhqa', and al-mushayya'ah. And al-musfarrah is that whose ear is uprooted until its ear-hole appears, and al-musta'salah is its horn uprooted from its root, and al-bakhqa' is that whose eye is gouged, and al-mushayya'ah is that which cannot follow the flock due to emaciation and weakness and incapacity, and al-kasra' is that which has no marrow. My father said:"
"17803","And Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to me: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it."
"17804","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from 'Utbah ibn 'Abd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate is in Quraysh, and judgment is among the Ansar, and the call is among the Abyssinians, and emigration is among the Muslims and the Muhajirun afterwards."
"17805","Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to us, or one who heard it narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Zayd al-Jawzajani narrated to me, he said: I went to the mosque and 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Mazini met me and said to me: Where are you going? I said: To the mosque. He said: Glad tidings, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no servant who leaves his house for a morning or evening going to the mosque except that his steps are: one step an expiation and one step a degree."
"17806","Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash informed us, from 'Aqil ibn Mudrik al-Sulami, from Luqman ibn 'Amir al-Wasabi, from 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for clothing, so he clothed me with two coarse cloths, and I indeed saw myself wearing them while I was among the best-clothed of his companions."
"17807","Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq, meaning al-Fazari, narrated to us, from Safwan, meaning Ibn 'Amr, from Abu al-Muthanna, from 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami, and he was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The slain are three: a believing man who fought with himself and his wealth in the path of Allah until when he met the enemy he fought them until he was killed; that is the boastful martyr in the tent of Allah beneath His Throne; the Prophets do not surpass him except by the degree of prophethood. And a believing man who burdened himself with sins and faults, who strove with himself and his wealth in the path of Allah until when he met the enemy he fought until he was killed; his sins and faults are wiped away, indeed the sword is a wiper of faults, and he will be admitted through whichever of the gates of Paradise he wishes, for it has eight gates, and for Hell there are seven gates, and some of them are better than others. And a hypocrite man who strove with himself and his wealth until when he met the enemy he fought in the path of Allah until he was killed; that one is in the Fire; the sword does not wipe away hypocrisy."
"17808","Ya'mar ibn Bishr narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Safwan ibn 'Amr informed us that Abu al-Muthanna al-Umluki narrated to him that he heard 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami, and he was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The slain are three, and he mentioned its meaning."
"17809","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd, he said: 'Utbah used to say: 'Irbad is better than me, and 'Irbad used to say: 'Utbah is better than me; he preceded me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a year."
